Neon: extension pages open in a global window, outside any space (M5).

With every space a container, the default storage partition is the global
context of the browser's own work and the extensions. Extension pages
(options pages, full-page UIs, chrome-extension:// URLs opened as tabs) need
it, so with spaces they open in a popup browser window centred on the active
browser window instead of in a tab of a space. A singleton navigation
(runtime.openOptionsPage) brings back the window that already shows the page.
A popup an extension opens with chrome.windows.create stays in the default
partition and is centred the same way when the extension gave no position.

The decision of what is global sits in chrome/browser/neon/global_context.h,
so the later overlay can reuse it. Hooks: one call in chrome::Navigate(), one
in WindowsCreateFunction. Without spaces (containers off) nothing changes.

